msgid "header.subtitle"
msgstr "please don't book meetings just to ask questions"

msgid "header.introduction"
msgstr "Imagine someone booking a meeting with you before even trying to explain what they need help with... 🤦‍♀️"

msgid "example.character.neutral.name"
msgstr "Tim"

msgid "example.character.neutral.avatar-description"
msgstr "Tim's Slack avatar"

msgid "example.character.bad.name"
msgstr "Keith"

msgid "example.character.bad.avatar-description"
msgstr "Keith's Slack avatar"

msgid "example.character.good.name"
msgstr "Dawn"

msgid "example.character.good.avatar-description"
msgstr "Dawn's Slack avatar"

msgid "example.bad.title"
msgstr "❌ Don't do this"

# the placeholder is the year, i.e. "2022"
msgid "example.bad.body"
msgstr ""
"Note that Keith could have got his answer minutes sooner without disrupting Tim's focus time. Tim could have answered when convenient, between tasks!\n"
"\n"
"People who book meetings for simple questions are generally trying to be thorough or ensure clarity - and that's great! But it's %1$s and we have email, instant messaging, and other async communication tools. **Booking a meeting forces someone to context switch** and breaks their flow, which is lost productivity (and kinda annoying).\n"
"\n"
"The same goes for booking meetings to:\n"
"\n"
"- Ask a quick status update\n"
"- Get a simple yes/no answer\n"
"- Request information that could be sent in an email\n"
"- Ask \"can we chat about X?\" without explaining X\n"
"- Discuss something that hasn't been documented yet\n"
"- etc.\n"
"\n"
"**Just ask the questions first!** 😫\n"

msgid "example.bad.message1.timestamp"
msgstr "2:15 PM"

msgid "example.bad.message1.body"
msgstr "Can we have a quick meeting?"

msgid "example.bad.reply1.timestamp"
msgstr "2:19 PM"

msgid "example.bad.reply1.body"
msgstr "Sure, what's it about?"

msgid "example.bad.message2.timestamp"
msgstr "2:20 PM"

# the typo here is intentional - please maintain a similar unprofessional tone!
msgid "example.bad.message2.body"
msgstr "I want to learn about Project Foo"

msgid "example.bad.reply2.timestamp"
msgstr "2:20 PM"

msgid "example.bad.reply2.body"
msgstr "I could've sent you the docs or answered a few questions - would've been faster mate 😅"

msgid "example.good.title"
msgstr "✅ Instead try this"

msgid "example.good.body"
msgstr ""
"If you want to be courteous, **you can still be polite and also ask your questions directly via email or message.**\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"- \"Hey! I want to learn about Project Foo. Can you point me to the docs or give me an overview?\"\n"
"- \"Hi there! I need help understanding the deployment process. Are there any guides, or can you explain the key steps?\"\n"
"- \"What's the status on those NFRs? Happy to jump on a call if it's too complex to explain in writing!\"\n"
"- etc.\n"
"\n"
"**Ask the questions first, and if it turns out to be complex, then book a meeting.** It may seem trivial, but this approach allows for **asynchronous communication**. The other party can answer when it's convenient for them, without breaking their focus. If the answer reveals complexity, you can always schedule a meeting then to discuss in detail.\n"
"\n"
"**Important:** When meetings get booked without any context, progress stops. Everyone waits for the meeting to happen before moving forward. By asking questions first, work can continue async, and meetings become focused discussions rather than starting points.\n"
"\n"
"It's totally fine to ask questions and also offer to book a meeting to discuss them, but people should assume questions can be answered without a meeting first. This allows everyone to work on their own time and maintain their focus.\n"
"\n"
"When done right - everyone's happy! 🎉\n"

msgid "example.good.message1.timestamp"
msgstr "2:15 PM"

msgid "example.good.message1.body"
msgstr "Hiya! I want to learn about Project Foo - any docs or overview you can share? Happy to jump on a call if it's easier to explain!"

msgid "example.good.reply1.timestamp"
msgstr "2:15 PM"

msgid "example.good.reply1.body"
msgstr "hey, check out confluence.com/project-foo - has everything you need!"

msgid "example.good.message2.timestamp"
msgstr "2:15 PM"

# "Ta" is colloquial British(/Australian/etc) for "thanks"
msgid "example.good.message2.body"
msgstr "Perfect, thanks! I'll read through it"

msgid "example.good.reply2.timestamp"
msgstr "2:16 PM"

msgid "example.good.reply2.body"
msgstr "👌 np"

msgid "footer.note"
msgstr "This is kinda only half serious (kinda 👀) so please don't get [mad](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xzpndHtdl9A) at the person who sent you here."

msgid "footer.warning"
msgstr "That said, if you see this site's URL as someone's status/bio, think twice before booking a meeting - try asking your question first!"

